! C22-6578 PROBLEM 1.7, "INTERRUPTION MEASURE", printed pp.9-10 (PDF
! 10-11), read in the ink.
!
!   "$IA contains the address 1000.0.  It is desired that when a $TS
!   interruption occurs the instruction counter contents should be
!   stored in the first 19 bits of location 2000.0 and the main program
!   is to be continued.  Write a code to effect this."
!
!   Method 1.  SLC, 1000.0 + 4.0
!       TSFIX  SIC, 2000.0; BR, 0
!
! The two printed statements are the deck's TSFIX, as printed, on one
! line.  The MAIN PROGRAM IS THE DECK'S; the page only says there is
! one.  It loads $IA, sets the interval timer to ONE (word 1 bits 0-18,
! Reference Manual printed p.34), enables, and counts down in a
! CB-to-itself until the timer has certainly gone off, then runs to the
! end.
!
! WHAT THE PAGE STATES, AND WHICH CHECK TESTS IT:
!
!  saved_ic    "stored in the first 19 bits of location 2000.0".  The
!              word starts holding a pattern in bits 19-63, which must
!              survive.  The counter stored is the CB's own address:
!              comment (b), "The instruction counter is not changed
!              during the execution of the free instruction".  The CB
!              branches to itself, so it is the next instruction to run.
!  count_ran   "the main program is to be continued": BR, 0, "branch
!              relative to zero", comment (b).  $4's count reached zero
!              and the code after the wait ran (PC=HALT).
!  ts_off      Reference Manual printed p.48: the indicator that
!              interrupted is turned off on entry.  BTS must not branch
!              after the wait.
!
!  COMMENT (d), run as a plain main-line program, because it makes three
!  separate claims about SIC:
!
!    "The SIC action is not performed unless the ensuing branch is
!    successful"                                    -- sic_not_taken
!    "...and even then it is performed AFTER the execution of the
!    branch.  Instructions such as SIC, $+ 0.32; B, ANYWH will lead to
!    a branch to ANYWH if the branch is executed.  The instruction
!    counter will not have time to alter the branch address before
!    execution."                                    -- sic_self, anywh
!
!  sic_self stores the counter in the B's own address field and must
!  still go to ANYWH.  anywh records that it arrived.
!
!  NOT TESTED: comment (c), "The interrupt system is not disabled during
!  the execution of the free instruction" -- that needs a second
!  interruption to arrive during the two-instruction window.

        SLC, 1000.0 + 4.0
TSFIX   SIC, 2000.0; BR, 0

        SLC, 1100.0
START   BD, $+.32
        LVI, $13, 1000.0
        SV, $13, $IA
        LX, $4, ONE
        SV, $4, 1.0
        BE, $+.32
        LCI, $4, 16000
WAIT    CB, $4, $
        SX, $4, FIN4
        BZTS, TSCLR
        Z, TSOFF
TSCLR   NOP

! --- comment (d) ---------------------------------------------------
! NOT TAKEN.  XF is off here, so BXF does not branch and the SIC must
! store nothing.
        SIC, SNOT
        BXF, BAD
! TAKEN, into its own branch address.  Word-aligned so the check can
! name the word: SICW holds this SIC in its left half and the B in its
! right half.
        CNOP
SICW    SIC, $+0.32
SELF    B, ANYWH
        B, BAD
ANYWH   Z, ARRIVED
HALT    BEW, $
BAD     B, BAD
        CNOP

        SLC, 1200.0
ONE     XW, (8).40, 0, 0
FIN4    DD(BU,64,8), 0
TSOFF   DD(BU,64,8), 7
SNOT    DD(BU,64,8), 5
ARRIVED DD(BU,64,8), 7

! The page's location.  Bits 19-63 hold a pattern that must survive.
        SLC, 2000.0
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)00001FFFFFFFFFFF

        END, START
